  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Seville's food is not difficult and it is not expensive, and a visitor
          can still spend a week here eating worse than the man at the next
          counter paying a third as much. The reason is almost never the choice of
          place. It is that eating in this city runs on a set of conventions —
          about quantity, about where you stand, about what hour it is
          acceptable to be hungry — that nobody writes down because everybody
          local absorbed them at eight years old.
        </p>
        <p>
          So this is a guide to the system rather than a list of restaurants. It
          explains how an order is actually placed, why the same dish costs three
          different amounts in the same room, what the words on a chalkboard mean,
          and above all what the clock is doing — because more visitor meals in
          Seville are ruined by turning up at the wrong hour than by any other
          cause. The restaurants come near the end, they are few, and they are
          labelled by how much anybody can actually vouch for them.
        </p>
        <p>
          That labelling matters enough to state at the front. Four kinds of thing
          appear below and they are kept separate throughout.
          <strong>What was eaten</strong> — thirteen restaurants and a bar across
          nine nights in late September and early October 2025, recorded on this
          site as <a href="/10-days-in-sevilla/">ten days using Seville as a
          base</a>. <strong>What was eaten <em>at</em> them</strong> — which, with
          one exception, the record does not preserve, so the dishes of exactly
          one kitchen are described here and no others.
          <strong>What is currently verifiable</strong> — market hours,
          institutions, the shape of the trade, checked in July 2026.
          And <strong>how the city eats</strong>, which is not anybody's opinion
          and takes up most of the page. Nothing has been invented to fill the gaps
          between them.
        </p>
      </div>
