  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter
      id="system"
      number="I."
      name="How Tapas Actually Works"
      epithet="Three sizes, three prices for the same plate, and a bill written on the counter in chalk"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        A tapa is not a dish. It is a quantity. Almost everything on a Sevillian
        bar's board exists at three sizes, and the size is the unit you are
        ordering in: a <strong>tapa</strong> is a saucer's worth, three or four
        mouthfuls, priced for a person who intends to have four or five of them
        across two bars; a <strong>media ración</strong> is a half plate, enough
        for two people to share alongside something else; a
        <strong>ración</strong> is a full plate and is a portion of dinner. The
        prices sit in three columns on the board or on the menu, and the whole
        skill of eating well in Seville consists of matching the size to what you
        actually want, which is usually smaller than a visitor's instinct.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second convention is about position, and it is money. In most
        traditional establishments the same food carries three prices depending on
        where you consume it — cheapest standing at the bar, more at a table
        inside, more again on the terrace. This is legal, normal, printed on the
        menu if you look, and never mentioned. Standing at a bar in Seville is not
        a hardship posture; it is where the food is freshest, the turnover
        fastest, and the barman's attention actually available.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third is about ordering itself, and it is the one that intimidates
        people. You do not wait to be seated, greeted or approached. You find a
        space at the bar, catch the barman's eye, and ask for a drink; the food
        order follows in the same breath or a minute later, in pieces, as you
        decide. Nothing is ordered all at once. A Sevillian evening at a good
        counter consists of a drink, one tapa, a conversation, another tapa, and
        then leaving for somewhere else — the movement between two or three bars
        being the point rather than a failure to settle. In the older places the
        running total is chalked on the wooden counter in front of you, and you
        pay at the end, which looks like a charming survival and is in fact the
        most efficient system anyone has devised for the purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        And the fourth: <strong>tapas in Seville are not free.</strong> The
        tradition of a complimentary tapa arriving with each drink is real and
        Andalusian, and it belongs to Granada, Jaén and Almería. A traveller
        arriving in Seville from
        <a href="/best-day-trips-from-seville/">a day trip to Granada</a> — where
        that convention is still very much alive — sometimes carries the
        expectation west and is quietly disappointed for a week. Here you order,
        and you pay, and the compensation is that the food is better.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Beat>You are not choosing dishes. You are choosing sizes, and where to stand.</Beat>

    <Chapter
      id="clock"
      number="II."
      name="The Clock"
      epithet="The section most likely to change your trip, and the one most guides bury at the bottom"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Seville does not eat late as a matter of style. It eats late because for
        much of the year the middle of the day is unusable and the evening does
        not begin until the stone has stopped radiating, and the timetable that
        results is not negotiable by tourists. Two figures are worth memorising:
        <strong>lunch begins at about two</strong>, and <strong>dinner begins at
        about nine</strong>. Not "can be had from". Begins.
      </p>
      <p>
        The consequence that catches visitors is the gap. From roughly half past
        four to eight in the evening, a large proportion of traditional bars are
        either shut or serving drinks only, their kitchens cold between services.
        A hungry traveller walking the centre at seven finds, quite reliably, that
        the places still serving hot food at that hour are the ones that exist for
        people who do not know the timetable — the terraces with laminated
        photographs and a man outside inviting you in. That is the mechanism by
        which perfectly sensible visitors end up eating the worst meal of their
        trip on their first night. It is not bad luck. It is a schedule.
      </p>
      <p>
        The correction is simple and worth building the day around. Eat your main
        meal at lunch, between two and half past three, when the
        <strong>menú del día</strong> — three courses, bread and a drink at a
        fixed price — is the best value available anywhere in Spain and is what
        the people around you are having. Then treat the evening as tapas: go out
        at nine, not seven, and expect to eat in two or three places rather than
        one. If nine feels impossibly late, eat at half past eight and accept a
        half-empty room; the food will be the same and the atmosphere will arrive
        about the time you are finishing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two smaller notes on the clock. <strong>Sunday evening is quiet</strong>
        and a great many kitchens are closed; the good move is a long Sunday lunch
        instead. And <strong>August empties the city</strong>: family-run bars
        close for two or three weeks and post a handwritten sign, which is
        unpredictable and worth a phone call if you have set your heart on
        somewhere.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="repertoire"
      number="III."
      name="What to Understand Before You Order"
      epithet="A dozen things that are genuinely Sevillian, and what each one actually is"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Andalusian cooking is poor cooking that got very good at what it had:
        pork, olive oil, chickpeas, bread, sherry and whatever the Atlantic sent
        up the river. The list below is not a list of recommendations. It is a
        vocabulary, so that a chalkboard stops being an obstacle.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Espinacas con garbanzos</strong> — spinach with chickpeas, cumin,
        paprika and often a little vinegar and fried bread, served hot in a small
        earthenware dish. It is the most Sevillian thing on any board, it is
        usually the cheapest, it descends from Moorish and Jewish cooking, and it
        is the single best test of a bar: a place that makes this well is not
        cutting corners anywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Salmorejo</strong> — the cold one, and not gazpacho. Tomato,
        bread, olive oil and garlic blended thick enough to coat a spoon, usually
        with chopped egg and jamón on top. Gazpacho is thinner and includes pepper
        and cucumber; salmorejo is richer, comes from Córdoba, and is what you
        want in July.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Carrillada</strong> — pork or beef cheek braised until it gives up
        entirely, in a dark sherry-based sauce. Ordered as a media ración it is one
        of the best-value plates in the city.
        <strong>Cola de toro</strong> is oxtail treated the same way and takes
        even longer; where it appears it is usually the kitchen's declaration of
        seriousness.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Solomillo al whisky</strong> — pork tenderloin in a sauce of
        garlic, lemon and whisky, which sounds like a mistake and is a Sevillian
        institution. Order it with bread and do not think about it too hard.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pescaíto frito</strong> — the small fried fish of the Andalusian
        coast, floured and dropped in very hot olive oil, eaten with fingers and
        lemon. It is a Cádiz and Málaga tradition that Seville does well because
        the river brought the fish up; the coastal version, eaten from a paper
        cone standing on a square, is described in
        <a href="/1-day-in-cadiz/">the Cádiz chronicle</a>.
        <strong>Tortillitas de camarones</strong> — lacy fritters of tiny whole
        shrimp — belong to the same coast and turn up on better Seville boards.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Jamón ibérico</strong>, and the grading printed on the card above
        every market counter: <em>bellota 100%</em> from pure-bred acorn-fed pigs,
        <em>bellota</em> from crossbred acorn-fed, <em>cebo de campo</em> from
        pasture-raised on feed, and <em>cebo</em> from feed alone. The differences
        are real and the price differences are steep. In a bar, a plate of the
        second grade is a better purchase than a small plate of the first.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Montadito</strong> and <strong>serranito</strong> — the first a
        small filled roll, the second a substantial sandwich of pork loin with
        fried green pepper and jamón, which is lunch rather than a tapa and is
        what Sevillians eat when they are actually hungry and in a hurry.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Queso payoyo</strong> — a goat and sheep cheese from the Sierra de
        Grazalema, aged, nutty, and on every serious counter in the city. And for
        the end of a meal, <strong>convent sweets</strong>: enclosed religious
        communities across Seville still sell biscuits, marmalades and
        egg-yolk <em>yemas</em> through a revolving wooden hatch, so that the buyer
        and the seller do not see each other. The hatch at the convent on Plaza de
        Pilatos, beside Casa de Pilatos, is the one recorded on this site, and the
        yemas bought there were very good.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="sherry"
      number="IV."
      name="Sherry, and the One Thing to Know About It"
      epithet="Not a dessert wine, not sweet, and served cold in a glass the size of a thought"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The most common misunderstanding about sherry in northern Europe is that
        it is sweet. Almost none of it is. It comes from the triangle of
        vineyards around <a href="/best-day-trips-from-seville/">Jerez de la
        Frontera</a>, an hour down the Atlantic line, and it is made by the solera
        method — a cascade of barrels in which older wine is continuously
        refreshed by younger, so that no bottle belongs to a single year and the
        cellar itself is the vintage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four names cover almost everything you will meet.
        <strong>Fino</strong> is bone dry, pale, aged under a veil of yeast, and
        served cold in a small glass; it is what you drink with olives, jamón and
        fried fish, and it is the correct first order in an old Seville bar.
        <strong>Manzanilla</strong> is the same idea made at Sanlúcar on the
        coast, where the sea air makes it saltier and lighter.
        <strong>Amontillado</strong> is a fino that lost its yeast veil and
        oxidised — darker, nuttier, still dry, and very good with the braised
        dishes. <strong>Oloroso</strong> never had the veil at all: dark, broad,
        dry unless sweetened. Only <strong>Pedro Ximénez</strong>, almost black
        and made from sun-dried grapes, is the sweet thing people expect all
        sherry to be, and it belongs at the end of the meal over ice cream.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical point is that fino is cheap, it is poured cold and fast, it
        goes with everything Andalusian, and ordering it marks you out as somebody
        who has been paying attention. On the documented stay behind this page,
        the memorable version of that was a glass of fino arriving at a table in a
        modern tapas bar without having been asked for — which is what happens
        when the kitchen knows what the dish needs better than the customer does.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="markets"
      number="V."
      name="The Market as the City's Larder"
      epithet="Where the trade still happens daily, and why a visitor should go even without a kitchen"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Seville's markets are not food halls with a heritage façade. They are
        working shops where the neighbourhood buys dinner, and they run on the
        neighbourhood's hours rather than a visitor's — which is precisely why
        they are worth an hour of a trip. Stand in one at half past eleven on a
        Tuesday and the argument this article has been making about the clock
        becomes visible: the queues are at the fish and the ham, the shoppers are
        buying for a meal three hours away, and nobody is holding a menu.
      </p>
    </div>

