    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        <strong>Mercado de Triana</strong> is the one to see. It stands at the
        Triana foot of the Puente de Isabel II on the foundations of the castle of
        San Jorge, holds around eighty stalls, and sells what a household actually
        needs: fruit and vegetables, meat, <em>chacinas</em> — the cured pork
        trade — pickles, spices, fish and shellfish. Its rhythm is the thing to
        get right. The <strong>produce and retail stalls trade Monday to
        Saturday, roughly nine to half past two, and are closed on Sundays and
        public holidays.</strong> The bars and counters inside keep longer hours,
        including Sunday afternoons, which means a Sunday visit gives you a
        handsome hall with the trade gone out of it. Go on a weekday morning and
        eat at one of the counters between the stalls, which serve what the stalls
        sell — the whole argument for a market breakfast in one sentence.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
