      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Half past eleven on a Saturday. The stall is hung as well as stacked, because floor space costs and ceiling space does not."
    />

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mercado-de-triana-morning-shoppers.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mercado-de-triana-morning-shoppers-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mercado-de-triana-morning-shoppers-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mercado-de-triana-morning-shoppers-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mercado-de-triana-morning-shoppers-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/mercado-de-triana-morning-shoppers.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="An aisle in the Mercado de Triana mid-morning: a stall of mangos, avocados marked aguacate extra ecológico Málaga 3'95, radishes, fennel and punnets of berries, with two shoppers of retirement age standing at the counter and further stalls and shoppers beyond."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Avocados from Málaga at 3,95 a kilo, in October 2025. The prices on these cards are the only euro figures in this article that anybody can actually stand behind."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Two others are worth knowing about.
        <strong>Mercado de Feria</strong>, in the north of the city on the street
        of the same name, is reputed to be the oldest market in Seville and is
        markedly less visited than Triana — a neighbourhood market with a fish bar
        in it, in a quarter where nobody is selling anything to tourists. And
        <strong>Mercado de la Encarnación</strong> sits underneath the Setas, in
        the same excavation as the Antiquarium's Roman remains, which produces the
        pleasing situation of buying tomatoes on top of a Roman street.
      </p>
      <p>
        A word on the newer breed. Seville also has renovated market halls
        converted into food courts — the Lonja del Barranco on the riverbank being
        the best known. They are pleasant, they open at hours the traditional trade
        does not, and they are not the same thing at all: a food court is a place
        to eat and a market is a place to shop. Both are legitimate. Only one of
        them tells you anything about the city.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="neighbourhoods"
      number="VI."
      name="Where the Barrios Differ at the Table"
      epithet="Four quarters, four kinds of evening, and one to avoid eating in altogether"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Seville is small enough that a visitor can eat in a different quarter
        every night without effort, and the quarters are genuinely different at
        the table. Where you sleep interacts with this, which is argued separately
        in <a href="/best-areas-to-stay-in-seville/">the guide to the six
        areas</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Centro and Alfalfa</strong> hold the densest and best
        concentration of tapas bars in the city. Around Plaza de la Alfalfa and
        Plaza del Salvador the crowd stands outdoors with a drink in one hand from
        nine until very late, and two streets back from either square the bars are
        smaller, older and full of people who work nearby. If you eat in one
        quarter on one night, eat here.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>San Lorenzo and the Alameda de Hércules</strong> are where the
        city's younger and more contemporary kitchens have gathered, and where the
        eating goes latest. The Alameda itself is a bar strip under plane trees;
        San Lorenzo behind it is quieter and holds several of the more serious
        modern tapas rooms. This is the quarter that most rewards a booking.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Triana</strong>, across the river, eats like a neighbourhood
        rather than a district: Calle San Jacinto and the lanes off it for
        everyday counters, Calle Betis facing the lit old city across the water
        for the evening. The market anchors the morning. The register here is
        traditional and the prices are lower than the near bank's.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>El Arenal</strong>, between the Cathedral and the river, keeps a
        run of old comedores near the bullring — the sort of place with a tiled
        dado, a television on in the corner and a menú del día chalked outside. It
        is the least fashionable eating in central Seville and among the most
        reliable.
      </p>
      <p>
        And <strong>Santa Cruz</strong> is the quarter to walk through rather than
        eat in. The lanes east of the Cathedral are beautiful and they contain the
        highest concentration of restaurants in the city that exist for people who
        will not come back. There are honourable exceptions and finding them takes
        local knowledge. Three streets north, in Alfalfa, no such knowledge is
        required.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="two-kinds"
      number="VII."
      name="The Old Bar and the New Kitchen"
      epithet="Two traditions that coexist without much friction, and how to tell which one you have walked into"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Seville runs two parallel food cultures and a good week uses both.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>traditional bar</strong> announces itself: a marble or zinc
        counter, hams on a stand, barrels or bottles of fino behind, a tiled dado,
        handwritten paper signs, sawdust or paper napkins on the floor, and the
        bill chalked on the wood. The menu has not changed in thirty years and does
        not need to. You stand, you order two or three things, you pay in cash or
        card without ceremony, and the whole transaction takes twenty minutes.
        Within this category the <strong>abacería</strong> is worth naming
        separately — a deli-tavern that sells the ham, cheese, tinned fish and
        wine it also serves you, so the shelves around the room are the menu.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>contemporary kitchen</strong> looks different: fewer covers,
        a short printed or chalked list that changes, one or two dishes the place
        is known for, and reservations. What it is doing is not fusion. It is
        Andalusian cooking with better technique and less pork fat — the same
        oxtail, the same chickpeas, the same fino, executed with an attention the
        old bars do not attempt. These are the places to book, and the places
        where a visitor is most likely to have the meal they remember.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rule of thumb that follows is short: <strong>book the sit-down
        kitchens, never the counters.</strong> Turning up unannounced at a good
        modern tapas room at nine on a Friday will not work. Turning up at an old
        counter is the only way anyone ever does it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="mistakes">Nine mistakes, in the order visitors make them</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        <strong>One.</strong> Eating dinner at seven, which selects for the
        restaurants that cater to people eating dinner at seven.
        <strong>Two.</strong> Expecting a free tapa with the drink, which is
        Granada's convention and not Seville's.
        <strong>Three.</strong> Ordering raciones when you wanted tapas, and
        arriving at the third dish already full.
        <strong>Four.</strong> Sitting on a terrace for counter food and paying a
        third more for it without noticing.
        <strong>Five.</strong> Eating all three courses in one place, when the
        local practice is two or three bars in an evening and one dish in each.
        <strong>Six.</strong> Eating in Santa Cruz because you are standing in
        Santa Cruz.
        <strong>Seven.</strong> Going to the market in the afternoon, when the
        produce stalls shut at about half past two and are closed altogether on
        Sundays.
        <strong>Eight.</strong> Treating lunch as the light meal. In Seville it is
        the substantial one, and the menú del día is the best value of the trip.
        <strong>Nine.</strong> Ordering sherry expecting it to be sweet, and
        concluding you do not like sherry.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="record">What one documented stay actually ate</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        This section is the record rather than a recommendation, and the
        distinction is the point. Across nine nights in late September and early
        October 2025, thirteen restaurants and one bar were eaten and drunk in;
        the full list, night by night, is in
        <a href="/10-days-in-sevilla/">the ten-day chronicle</a> and is not
        reproduced here. Seven are named below because each one illustrates a
        category this article has been describing. All of them were open in 2025.
        None has been re-verified as trading today, and a reader planning to cross
        the city for one should check first.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Eslava</strong>, on the street of the same name behind San
        Lorenzo, is the modern-tapas category in a single address: a place three
        separate people insisted on, serious about ingredients and unserious about
        atmosphere, whose slow-cooked egg has been famous for years and whose
        oxtail croquette deserves to be. It is also the one kitchen on this page
        whose dishes the record preserves — the egg, the croquette, and a glass of
        fino that arrived without being asked for. Everything else eaten on that
        stay is a name and a night, and this article does not invent the plates.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Abacería del Postigo</strong>, near the river in El Arenal, is
        what an abacería is: ham, cheese, natural wine, and no ceremony at all.
        <strong>Baratillo</strong>, on Calle Adriano beside the bullring, is the
        old comedor category — river-facing, traditional, full of locals, and the
        most Sevillian lunch on the recorded list.
        <strong>Taberna la Auténtica Regina</strong>, three minutes from Casa de
        Pilatos, is the cheap noisy neighbourhood counter that a good day needs
        somewhere in the middle of it.
        <strong>Balbuena y Huertas</strong> on Calle San Jacinto is Triana eating
        late, on the far bank, after something else.
        <strong>El Disparate</strong> on the Alameda de Hércules is the
        contemporary Alameda register — a corner site with the name painted large
        on the wall and tables out on the pavement.
      </p>
      <p>
        And <strong>El Garlochi</strong> on Calle Boteros is not food at all but
        belongs here anyway: a bar decorated wholly in the imagery of the Semana
        Santa brotherhoods, smelling of incense, serving cocktails with names that
        are either blasphemous or devout depending on your temperament. It is the
        best single illustration in the city of how completely the religious
        calendar has soaked into ordinary Sevillian life, and it is open late.
      </p>
    </div>

