    <Practical place="Seville — Eating" detail="Verified 29 July 2026 · research, not recollection" detailIcon="🍽">
      <PracticalSection label="Currently verifiable: the markets" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Mercado de Triana</strong>, Calle San Jorge — retail and produce
          stalls <strong>Monday to Saturday, roughly 09:00–14:30, closed Sundays
          and public holidays</strong>. Bars and counters inside run longer, Monday
          to Saturday roughly 09:00–17:00 and 18:00–00:00, and Sundays and
          holidays 10:00–17:00. Around eighty stalls. Bring some cash for the
          stalls; the counters take cards.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Mercado de Feria</strong>, Calle Feria — a neighbourhood market
          in the north of the city, reputed to be the oldest in Seville, with a
          fish bar attached. Morning trade, and far quieter than Triana.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Mercado de la Encarnación</strong> — beneath the Setas, sharing
          its excavation with the Antiquarium's Roman remains.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Convent sweets</strong> — sold through a revolving hatch at
          enclosed communities across the city, including the convent on Plaza de
          Pilatos next to Casa de Pilatos. Ring the bell at the hatch.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="The rules worth carrying" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Order by size, not by dish.</strong> Tapa, media ración,
          ración. Two tapas each is a light dinner; two raciones each is too much
          food.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Stand at the bar.</strong> It is cheaper than a table and the
          table is cheaper than the terrace, in the same room, for the same food.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Book the modern kitchens; never book a counter.</strong> A day
          or two ahead is enough except at weekends.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Order fino, cold, first.</strong> It is inexpensive, dry, and
          right with almost everything on the board.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Use lunch.</strong> The menú del día between two and half past
          three is the best-value meal in Spain and the one the city itself is
          eating.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="What this page does not claim" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>No restaurant here has been verified as currently trading.</strong>
          The seven named are drawn from a documented stay in September and
          October 2025. Sevillian bars close for August and for family reasons at
          short notice; check before making a journey.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>No dish is described at any venue except Eslava</strong>, because
          the record does not preserve what was eaten elsewhere and inventing it
          would make the rest of this page worthless.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>No prices are quoted for any restaurant.</strong> The only euro
          figures on the page are the ones legible on market price cards in
          October 2025.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          The market hours above were checked against the market's own published
          timetable in July 2026. Everything else in this practical block is
          either structural or explicitly framed as reputation rather than
          verification.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>

    <PullQuote>
      A tapa is not a dish. It is a quantity — and almost every visitor's first
      bad meal in Seville is really an error of arithmetic, committed two hours
      too early in the evening.
    </PullQuote>
  </div>

  <div class="post-closing">
    <div class="narrative prose" style="max-width:820px; margin:0 auto;">
      <p>
        What the food actually tells you about Seville is that the city is
        organised around heat and company, in that order. The long lunch and the
        late dinner are not indulgences; they are what you arrive at after several
        centuries of afternoons too hot to work in. The small plate exists so that
        an evening can be spent in three rooms rather than one. The bill chalked on
        the counter exists because nobody was ever meant to sit down long enough to
        need a receipt. Even the sherry is a solution to a problem — a wine that
        will not spoil in a hot cellar, poured cold and drunk fast.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which is why the useful instruction at the end of a food guide is not a
        restaurant name. It is to stop resisting the timetable. Eat at two and at
        nine, order less than you think, stand up, and let the evening take you
        through more than one door. Do that and Seville feeds you extremely well
        for very little, in the same rooms and at the same hours as the people who
        live here — which is the whole of what anybody means by eating well in a
        foreign city. The longer record of one stay that did exactly that is in
        <a href="/10-days-in-sevilla/">the ten-day chronicle</a>; the rest of the
        table, from Bologna to Tangier, is in <a href="/chronicles/">the
        archive</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

